1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,020
Why do I always have to carry the heavy stuff?

2
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:07,850
Well, it's very simple.

3
00:00:07,890 --> 00:00:10,950
In our ragtag band of scientists with nothing to lose,

4
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:12,390
I'm the smart one,

5
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:13,750
Wolowitz is the funny one,

6
00:00:13,790 --> 00:00:15,850
and Koothrappali is the lovable foreigner

7
00:00:15,890 --> 00:00:18,350
who struggles to understand our ways and fails.

8
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:23,090
That leaves you, by default, as the muscle.

9
00:00:24,390 --> 00:00:27,450
One more floor, and I'd be the pulled muscle.

10
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:31,050
Oh, it's about time. I'm starving.

11
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:33,620
Um, well, we didn't actually get Chinese food.

12
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:36,250
- Why not?  - Don't panic. This is better.

13
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:39,750
Oh, no. You didn't trade the food for magic beans, did you?

14
00:00:39,790 --> 00:00:42,390
Of course not.

15
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:44,250
And technically, magic beans would be food.

16
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:46,390
Although eating them would be quite a waste,

17
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:47,750
since you could plant them,

18
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:49,320
and overnight, have a giant beanstalk,

19
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:52,220
which would provide enough roughage for a small city.

20
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:54,450
Yeah, sometimes I don't listen.

21
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:57,150
Sometimes I just watch your jaw go up and down.

22
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,320
We were on our way to the Chinese restaurant

23
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:01,620
when we thought we saw Adam West,

24
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:02,690
so we followed him.

25
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:03,820
Who's Adam West?

26
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:06,020
Who's Adam West?!

27
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:09,320
Leonard, what do the two of you talk about after the coitus?

28
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:14,520
My guess is, "Hey, four minutes! New record!"

29
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:19,650
That's why I'm the funny one.

30
00:01:19,690 --> 00:01:22,120
Anyway, we followed the guy to this garage sale,

31
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:23,690
and they had the coolest stuff.

32
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:24,790
They were closing up.

33
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:26,650
We got this whole box for 60 bucks.

34
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:28,050
We didn't even get to go through it all.

35
00:01:28,090 --> 00:01:29,350
There could be anything in here.

36
00:01:29,390 --> 00:01:32,120
There a new girlfriend in there? 'Cause you might need one.

37
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:33,950
No.

38
00:01:33,990 --> 00:01:37,320
But there is an original final draft Ghostbusters script

39
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:39,050
with actual slime stains!

40
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:41,550
Oh, you're right.

41
00:01:41,590 --> 00:01:42,790
It's Ghostbusters 2.

42
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:44,050
Never mind.

43
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,500
Oh, my God.

44
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:53,030
An Alf doll.

45
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:56,380
When I was 11, my mother got me one

46
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:58,660
to help me sleep after my dad left.

47
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:02,900
I used to pretend that my dad had moved to the planet Melmac,

48
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:07,330
and Alf was going to bring him back to me.

49
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,630
But he never did.

50
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,860
Where's my daddy, puppet?

51
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,500
Where is he?

52
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:21,360
That is so sad.

53
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,560
No, what's sad is that you don't know

54
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:26,530
Adam West was TV's Batman.

55
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:55,030
Here's Spock's head with no body.

56
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:59,700
Here's Mr.T's body with no head.

57
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,700
Oh, yeah, here's Spock's body with Mr.T's head.

58
00:03:06,470 --> 00:03:08,970
I pity the fool who's illogical.

59
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:13,930
Okay, I'm just gonna go home

60
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:17,770
and make a grilled cheese and window-shop on eHarmony.

61
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:20,700
Okay, bye.

62
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:22,300
Okay.

63
00:03:22,330 --> 00:03:25,730
Ooh, look, an Indiana Jones connect-the-dots.

64
00:03:25,770 --> 00:03:27,900
And an Aquaman action figure.

65
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:30,200
Looks like someone drew a penis on him.

66
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:32,400
Ooh, that'll come off.

67
00:03:41,270 --> 00:03:43,600
You see what you're doing?

68
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:46,070
Stop that.

69
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:47,330
Fascinating.

70
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:48,770
What?

71
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,200
It appears to be a Lord of the Rings ring.

72
00:03:51,230 --> 00:03:52,700
Oh. It's even got

73
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:54,270
the Elvish engraving on it.

74
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:55,600
It's not Elvish.

75
00:03:55,630 --> 00:03:58,830
It's the language of Mordor written in Elvish script.

76
00:04:00,170 --> 00:04:03,000
"One Ring to rule them all."

77
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,600
"One Ring to find them."

78
00:04:05,630 --> 00:04:08,200
"One Ring to bring them all."

79
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:11,730
"And in the darkness bind them."

80
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:15,700
Holy crap, are we nerdy.

81
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:20,030
So, I was doing some checking on the ring.

82
00:04:20,070 --> 00:04:22,270
Hold on. Sheldon, is there ketchup on that table?

83
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:24,530
Yes, there is.

84
00:04:24,570 --> 00:04:29,100
Oh, here's a fun fact. Ketchup started out as a general term for sauce,

85
00:04:29,130 --> 00:04:32,400
typically made of mushrooms or fish brine with herbs and spices.

86
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:35,130
Some popular early main ingredients included blueberry,

87
00:04:35,170 --> 00:04:38,630
anchovy, oyster, kidney bean and grape.

88
00:04:39,930 --> 00:04:42,600
No, that's okay. I'll get it.

89
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:47,200
Listen, I was looking at the ring, and it seemed a little weird.

90
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:48,770
No copyright notice on it.

91
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:52,300
So, I took it down to this buddy of mine who deals with--

92
00:04:52,330 --> 00:04:55,900
shall we say-- the seedy underbelly of the collectibles world.

93
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:57,370
Seedy underbelly?

94
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:59,730
You know, your black market phasers,

95
00:04:59,770 --> 00:05:01,900
your screen-worn Lieutenant Uhura panties,

96
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:03,270
that kind of stuff.

97
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:07,470
Who's this mysterious buddy you suddenly have?

98
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,530
Just a guy. I know a guy.

99
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:12,230
- Is it Eddie Crispo? - No, I can't tell you who it is. Stop asking.

100
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:13,870
Who else could it be? It has to be Eddie Crispo.

101
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:15,970
I know lots of dangerous people, okay?

102
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,030
- Name one. - Eddie Crispo.

103
00:05:19,070 --> 00:05:25,170
Anyway, he said this isn't a replica. It's the real deal.

104
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:28,130
If you're suggesting that that is the actual ring of power

105
00:05:28,170 --> 00:05:30,400
forged by Sauron in Mount Doom,

106
00:05:30,430 --> 00:05:32,830
I look at you with an expression of exhaustion

107
00:05:32,870 --> 00:05:34,870
and ever so slight amusement.

108
00:05:37,970 --> 00:05:40,830
He's not saying it's a magic ring.

109
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:42,770
You're not, are you?

110
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,800
No, but it's close.

111
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:46,300
Look at the markings inside.

112
00:05:46,330 --> 00:05:48,600
Those are production markings.

113
00:05:48,630 --> 00:05:51,570
Nine rings were made for use in the Lord of the Rings movies.

114
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:53,400
Three were given to members of the cast.

115
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:54,870
The rest were destroyed.

116
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:56,630
Except one.

117
00:05:56,670 --> 00:05:59,370
One was stolen.

118
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:01,430
Gentlemen...

119
00:06:01,470 --> 00:06:05,000
this is the one ring.

120
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:09,830
Mine!

121
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:12,430
No, it is not yours.

122
00:06:12,470 --> 00:06:14,070
We all went in on the box together.

123
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,400
Well, yes, but I found it in the box,

124
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:18,500
and the laws of maritime salvage clearly state

125
00:06:18,530 --> 00:06:20,400
that the finder of a sunken treasure

126
00:06:20,430 --> 00:06:22,000
is the owner of the treasure.

127
00:06:22,030 --> 00:06:23,900
How is this maritime salvage?

128
00:06:23,930 --> 00:06:26,330
Other than the lack of water, how is it not?

129
00:06:26,370 --> 00:06:28,870
Wait, wait. Sheldon, stop being crazy for a second.

130
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:31,070
How much is something like this worth?

131
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:33,700
Well, it's tough to say since it's hot,

132
00:06:33,730 --> 00:06:35,800
but on the underground market, my guy figures...

133
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:36,830
Your guy Eddie Crispo?

134
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:39,030
Yes.

135
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:44,900
He figures ten, maybe 15 thousand.

136
00:06:44,930 --> 00:06:46,400
Okay, that's a lot of money.

137
00:06:46,430 --> 00:06:49,530
The wise thing to do is invest it in something practical.

138
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:51,270
Like a jet ski.

139
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:54,230
Why do you want a jet ski?

140
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:57,570
All the wealthy and beautiful people in movies ride Jet Skis.

141
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:59,370
That can't just be a coincidence.

142
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:01,470
We can't sell it.

143
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,070
We have to keep it

144
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:04,400
and love it and polish it,

145
00:07:04,430 --> 00:07:05,870
and only take it out occasionally

146
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:07,200
when we go to the park

147
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:10,200
and reenact our favorite scenes from the movies.

148
00:07:10,230 --> 00:07:14,270
It's sad how great that sounds.

149
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,470
Guys, it's stolen.

150
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,100
It should go back to Peter Jackson.

151
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:18,930
He made the movies; it belongs to him.

152
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:20,130
Fine.

153
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:22,500
He can have it back as long as he promises

154
00:07:22,530 --> 00:07:24,770
to make me a hobbit in his next movie.

155
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:26,870
There are no Jewish hobbits.

156
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:28,730
Clearly, you've never been to my house

157
00:07:28,770 --> 00:07:31,000
for dinner on Rosh Hashanah.

158
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:33,400
We are not blackmailing Peter J...

159
00:07:33,430 --> 00:07:34,900
All right, where's the ring?

160
00:07:34,930 --> 00:07:37,200
You mean my ring?

161
00:07:37,230 --> 00:07:38,300
What are you doing?

162
00:07:38,330 --> 00:07:39,470
The ring was unguarded.

163
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:41,230
It was just sitting on the table.

164
00:07:41,270 --> 00:07:42,700
Anyone could have taken it.

165
00:07:42,730 --> 00:07:44,630
Proof? I did.

166
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:47,200
- Give me that. - Look, let's be reasonable.

167
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:49,570
We all want to do different things with the ring,

168
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:52,500
but your ideas are stupid, and I want a jet ski.

169
00:07:52,530 --> 00:07:54,230
I found it. The ring is mine.

170
00:07:54,270 --> 00:07:57,570
I don't understand why in this group I never get my way.

171
00:08:00,970 --> 00:08:04,130
You always get your way.

172
00:08:04,170 --> 00:08:08,430
I'll stipulate to that if you give me the ring.

173
00:08:08,470 --> 00:08:09,600
Hey, guys.

174
00:08:09,630 --> 00:08:10,970
Enjoying your food that I actually

175
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,630
brought you instead of promising food,

176
00:08:12,670 --> 00:08:15,770
but bringing you a box of random crap?

177
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:16,900
Yeah, it's delicious.

178
00:08:16,930 --> 00:08:18,730
The sarcasm's a little stale, though.

179
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:21,030
Hey, how about this?

180
00:08:21,070 --> 00:08:22,900
Until we figure out what to do with the ring,

181
00:08:22,930 --> 00:08:24,070
Penny holds on to it.

182
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:24,970
What ring?

183
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,130
This ring.

184
00:08:27,170 --> 00:08:29,300
Looking for something?

185
00:08:31,530 --> 00:08:33,830
Will you hold on to this for a couple of days?

186
00:08:33,870 --> 00:08:35,470
- Why? - It's a prop from a movie,

187
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:37,430
And we're kind of fighting over it.

188
00:08:37,470 --> 00:08:39,130
Okay, just to be clear, the first piece of jewelry

189
00:08:39,170 --> 00:08:40,870
my boyfriend gives me is a prop from a movie,

190
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:42,370
and I don't even get to keep it?

191
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:46,070
If you had gone out with me three years ago,

192
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:48,500
by now, you'd have my great Aunt Ida's brooch

193
00:08:48,530 --> 00:08:52,070
that she smuggled out of occupied Belgium in a cat.

194
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:55,370
How am I looking now?

195
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:00,330
You hit me!

196
00:10:00,370 --> 00:10:03,530
I'm bleeding!

197
00:10:04,470 --> 00:10:06,230
What was that?

198
00:10:06,270 --> 00:10:08,500
Sheldon tried to take the ring and I punched him.

199
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,900
That's my girl.

200
00:10:18,330 --> 00:10:20,000
So, Sheldon,

201
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,160
how's it feel to get beaten up by a girl?

202
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:27,960
It's not the first time.

203
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,400
I have a twin sister whose assaults began in utero.

204
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:35,530
If only I'd had the presence of mind to reabsorb her,

205
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:37,560
then I'd have a mole with hair in it

206
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:41,000
instead of a tedious yearly Christmas letter.

207
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:42,930
Gentlemen, have you come to the realization

208
00:10:42,970 --> 00:10:44,770
that the only reasonable course of action

209
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:46,570
is to sell the ring and divide the money?

210
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:47,730
No.

211
00:10:47,770 --> 00:10:48,800
I was afraid of that.

212
00:10:50,870 --> 00:10:52,800
Go ahead.

213
00:10:52,830 --> 00:10:54,430
Greetings from Mumbai.

214
00:10:54,470 --> 00:10:56,930
I am Raj's attorney, Venkatesh Koothrappali.

215
00:10:56,970 --> 00:10:58,970
Also my cousin.

216
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,430
You brought a lawyer?

217
00:11:00,470 --> 00:11:01,670
Don't answer that.

218
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:03,830
I'll get straight to the point.

219
00:11:03,870 --> 00:11:05,870
My client's prepared to surrender

220
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:07,330
any interest he has in the ring

221
00:11:07,370 --> 00:11:10,070
in exchange for two Kawasaki Jet Skis.

222
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:13,100
We're not giving him two Jet Skis.

223
00:11:13,130 --> 00:11:14,530
Look, we're big boys,

224
00:11:14,530 --> 00:11:17,230
why don't we just cut to the chase and meet in the middle?

225
00:11:17,230 --> 00:11:19,670
One Kawasaki Jet Ski. Done and done.

226
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:24,300
No Jet Skis.

227
00:11:24,630 --> 00:11:26,730
All right, forget the Jet Skis.

228
00:11:26,770 --> 00:11:29,500
Forget the Jet Skis? That was our line in the sand!

229
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:31,400
What happened to "tear them a new one?"

230
00:11:31,430 --> 00:11:35,230
What can I say? They played hardball. We lost.

231
00:11:35,270 --> 00:11:36,570
You're useless.

232
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:38,400
I told you that when you hired me.

233
00:11:38,430 --> 00:11:39,870
I'm signing off now.

234
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:43,030
Call your mother. She worries.

235
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:47,210
Okay, just so you know, if we're bringing in cousins who are lawyers,

236
00:11:47,210 --> 00:11:50,900
prepare for shock and awe.

237
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:52,280
You know what? I am ending this.

238
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:53,590
Penny didn't want to hold the ring anymore.

239
00:11:53,590 --> 00:11:56,460
She gave it to me. I have it. I'm sending it back.

240
00:11:56,490 --> 00:11:58,190
Where's the ring?

241
00:11:58,230 --> 00:12:00,660
You mean this ring?

242
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:03,460
Next time be aware of your surroundings

243
00:12:03,490 --> 00:12:05,660
while urinating in a public men's room.

244
00:12:05,690 --> 00:12:06,860
Give me that.

245
00:12:06,890 --> 00:12:07,890
No, it's mine.

246
00:12:07,930 --> 00:12:08,830
It's all of ours.

247
00:12:11,030 --> 00:12:12,830
- Okay, now, this is ridiculous!  - Then let go!

248
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:14,190
I'm not letting go. You let go.

249
00:12:14,230 --> 00:12:15,660
I say this ring belongs

250
00:12:15,690 --> 00:12:17,730
to the last person who can hold on.

251
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:19,930
Fine. But can't we go home and start this?

252
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:22,460
Sure. Let go of the ring.

253
00:12:22,490 --> 00:12:24,990
All right, it starts now.

254
00:12:28,990 --> 00:12:31,890
You do realize there's a giant bug movie marathon

255
00:12:31,930 --> 00:12:34,160
tonight on the Syfy Channel.

256
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:50,960
Wait, my laptop.

257
00:13:01,630 --> 00:13:04,560
You know, there's a point no when this becomes idiotic.

258
00:13:04,590 --> 00:13:07,830
And it wasn't when we were driving like this?

259
00:13:09,090 --> 00:13:11,060
I would advise the three of you

260
00:13:11,090 --> 00:13:12,690
that resistance is futile.

261
00:13:12,730 --> 00:13:14,360
I have endless patience.

262
00:13:14,390 --> 00:13:16,590
I once spent two-and-a-half hours on hold

263
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:18,590
with Hewlett-Packard customer service

264
00:13:18,630 --> 00:13:21,760
just to complain about their customer service.

265
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:25,260
You want to talk about endless patience?

266
00:13:25,290 --> 00:13:27,290
Penny made me watch all five seasons

267
00:13:27,330 --> 00:13:29,460
